      "textEn": "Every day, on the three sacred vows, you place the virtuous souls on the supremely auspicious path and inspire them\nto perform religious deeds, so that they attain spiritual status. Meaning: According to Vedic culture, the three upper castes of society (intellectual, political, and\nmerchant class) are associated with the guru through initiation, and they receive the Gayatri Mantra. This pure\nmantra is recited three times a day—during sunrise, noon, and sunset. To perform spiritual deeds, the auspicious time is calculated according to the path of the sun in the sky.\nAnd here, the credit for this well-organized spiritual work is given to the sun, the representative form of God.\nThe fear that arises from the sages who live in the forest, the hope that is fulfilled by the guardians of the forest, there, there.\nThe one who is pleased with lotus-like hands. 71. Meaning of the word\n\nAvani-patih - King; Iva - like; Asadhunaam - of the impure; Bhayam - fear; Udhirayan - while generating; Atati -\nHaving wandered here and there; Paritah - all around; Ashapaala - by the lords of directions; Tatra Tatra - there and there;\nKamal-kosha - holding lotus flowers; Anjalibhih - with folded hands; Upahit - offered; Arhana - offerings..\n\nYou, like the king of the earth, wander everywhere, spreading fear among the wicked,\nand the powerful gods of the directions, with folded hands, offer you lotus flowers and other\nrespectful offerings.\nNow, I bow to the lotus feet of the Lord, who is worshipped by the three worlds' gurus. I offer my prayers. 72. Meaning of the word\n\nAth—in this way; Ha—indeed; Bhagavan—Oh Lord; Tava—yours; Charan-nalin-yugalam—two lotus feet; Tri-bhuvan—of the three worlds; Guru-bhishah—by the Gurus; Abhivanditam—honored; Aham—I; Ayat-yam—unknown to anyone else;\nYaju-kamah—desirous of obtaining the Yajur mantra; Upasarami—I am coming near with worship; Iti—Thus..\nTherefore, O Lord, while praising you, I want to reach those feet of yours