      "textEn": "Thus spoke Goswami; thus—in this way; Stuta—praised; Sah—He; Bhagavan—powerful; Devata—deity; Vaji-rupa—in the form of a horse; Dharah—by assuming; Ravi—Sun God; Yajunshi—Yajur mantra; Ayat-yamani—other;\nNothing was learned from any mortal being; Munay—to the sage; Adaat—offered; Prasaditah—being pleased. Sut Goswami said: Pleased by such praise, the mighty Sun God assumed the form of a horse\nand granted the Yajur mantras to Yajnavalkya Muni, which were previously unknown in human society. Yajurvairbhirkochaaksha dasha pancha shatairvibhuh. Jagrahurvajasanyastah kanvamadhinadayah. 74. Meaning of the word\n\nYajurbhishah—From the Yajur mantras; Akarot—Made; Shakhaah—Branches; Dashah—Ten; Panchah—Five; Shatah—In hundreds; Vibhuh—\nPowerful; Jagruhah—They accepted; Vajasanyah—Born from the horse's umbrella, hence known as Vajasaneeya;\nTahah—To them; Kanva-Madhyandina-Adayah—Disciples of Kanva and Adhyandina, etc., sages.\nFrom these hundreds of mantras of the Yajurveda, the powerful sage created fifteen new\nbranches of Vedic literature. These became famous by the name Vajasaneya Samhita because they were born from the Ayala of horses,\nand they were accepted by the disciples of Kanva, Madhyandina, and other sages in the disciple-\ntradition. Jaimine: Samagasya samantu stanayo muniha. Sutvamastu tat sutastabhyam ekam prah samhitam. 75. Meaning of the word\n\nJaimineh - Of Jaimini; Sam-gasya - The singer of Samaveda; Aasit - Was; Sumanthuha - Sumanthu; Tanayah - Son; Muniha - Muni\n(Jaimini) of; Sutvan - Sutvan; Tu - Tatha; Tat-Sutah - The son of Sumanthu; Tabhyam - Of them; Eka-ekam -\nOne name out of two parts; Praah - He spoke; Samhitam - Compilation..\n\nThe author of the Samaveda was the son of Jaimini Rishi named Sumanthu, and Sumanthu's son\nwas Sutvan. Jaimini told each of them the different parts of Samaveda hymns. Sukarma, having touched, followed the great Samaveda.\nThe thousand-limbed one, having made a distinction, then the twice-born. 