 त्रि-वृतः—तीन प्रकार\nका; अहमः—मिथ्या अहंकार से; भूत-सूक्ष्म—अनुभूति के सूक्ष्म रूपों की; इन्द्रिय—इन्द्रियों की; अर्थानाम्—तथा\nइन्द्रिय-विषयों की; सम्भवः—उत्पत्ति; सर्गः—सृष्टि; उच्यते—कहलाती है।.",
      "textEn": "Creation (Sarga), then the creation of worlds and beings (Visarga), the nurturing of all beings,\ntheir sustenance (Raksha), the rule of various Manus (Antarani), the lineage of great kings,\nthe activities of such kings (Vanshanucharit), destruction (Sansthana), cause (Hetu),\nand supreme refuge (Apashraya). Other scholars say that the Mahapuranas contain the description of these ten,\nwhile the smaller Puranas contain only five. Meaning: In the second canto (2.10.1) of Shrimad Bhagavatam, the ten subjects of the Mahapurana have also been described—\n\nSrishuka said,\nHere are the ten subjects, the ten cantos, and the ten places of nourishment. The story of Manvantara is the cessation of the cycle of birth and liberation. “Shri Shukdev Goswami said: In Shrimad Bhagwat, there are ten types of\nstatements regarding the following: Creation of the universe, prepositions, worlds, protection by God, creative inspiration, transformation of humans, divine science, return to the abode of God, salvation, and supreme refuge.\n”\n\nAccording to Srila Jeeva Goswami, in Puranas like Srimad Bhagavat, these ten subjects are described,\nwhile in smaller Puranas, only five subjects are mentioned. It is said in Vedic literature—\nSargas, parishargas, vanshas, and Manvantaras. Vanshanucharitam cheti puranam panchalakshanam. “Creation, secondary creation, dynasties, the kingdoms of Manu, and the activities of various dynasties—these are the five characteristics of the Puranas.” Those Puranas in which five types of knowledge reside are called secondary Puranic literature.\nThey are called. Srila Jeeva Goswami has explained that the ten main subjects of Srimad Bhagavatam are found in each of the twelve cantos. One should not try to determine a subject for a single scindh. Nor should one try to show by artificial discourse of Srimad Bhagavatam\nthat these subjects have been described one after another.\nThe straightforward thing is that\nall aspects of knowledge that are 